Q: How do I make it so that the user only enters numbers in Console.ReadLine? A: <p>You can extract a method for such inputs:</p>
<pre><code>using System.Globalization;

...

public static T ReadParsable&lt;T&gt;(string prompt) where T : IParsable&lt;T&gt; {
  while (true) {
    if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(prompt))
      Console.Write(prompt);

    if (T.TryParse(Console.ReadLine(), CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, out var result))
      return result;

    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Invalid format, please, try again.&quot;);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and then use it as follow:</p>
<pre><code>int x = ReadParsable&lt;int&gt;(&quot;Please, provide int &gt; &quot;);
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/NHMfDH" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Fiddle</a></p>

Q: How do I correctly handle `XCB_RESIZE_REQUEST` events? A: <p>You need to switch on <code>XCB_CONFIGURE_NOTIFY</code> instead of <code>XCB_RESIZE_REQUEST</code>. <code>XCB_RESIZE_REQUEST</code> is sent when a resize is requested, and <code>XCB_CONFIGURE_NOTIFY</code> is sent when the window configuration has changed; that is when the <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> fields should contain the new window inside dimensions.</p>
<h3>Why am I now getting events of type 150?</h3>
<p>The posted code does not mask off the high bit of the event codes. <a href="https://www.x.org/releases/current/doc/xproto/x11protocol.html#event_format" rel="nofollow noreferrer">X11 sets the high bit to indicate that an event was generated by <code>SendEvent</code>.</a> In order to reliably detect events you need to mask this high bit:</p>
<pre><code>switch (event-&gt;response_type &amp; ~0x80) {
 case XCB_CONFIGURE_NOTIFY: {
   const xcb_configure_notify_event_t e = *(xcb_configure_notify_event_t *)event;
   cairo_xcb_surface_set_size(main_surface, e.width, e.height);
 }
 default:
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/60744214/what-does-the-0x80-mean-in-libxcb-code-sample">another Q&amp;A</a> that discusses this masking issue further.</p>
<p>You will also need to change the call to <code>xcb_create_window()</code>, setting the <code>StructureNotifyMask</code> bit of the window to subscribe to <code>ConfigureNotify</code> events:</p>
<pre><code>xcb_create_window(
                  connection, XCB_COPY_FROM_PARENT, main_window, screen-&gt;root,
                  100, 100, start_width, start_height, 0,
                  XCB_WINDOW_CLASS_INPUT_OUTPUT,
                  screen-&gt;root_visual,
                  XCB_CW_BACK_PIXEL | XCB_CW_EVENT_MASK,
                  (uint32_t []){screen-&gt;black_pixel, XCB_EVENT_MASK_STRUCTURE_NOTIFY}
                  );
</code></pre>
<h3>Magic Numbers and Readability</h3>
<p>Note that it may be possible to use <code>XCB_EVENT_RESPONSE_TYPE_MASK</code> instead of the magic number <code>~0x80</code>. This is a macro defined in the <code>xcb_event.h</code> header, but isn't always available. The <code>xcb_event.h</code> header is typically part of the <code>xcb-utils</code> library. For projects that do not wish to include the <code>xcb-utils</code> dependency, it is typical to use <code>~0x80</code> or <code>0x7f</code> (which is how <code>XCB_EVENT_RESPONSE_TYPE_MASK</code> is defined).</p>
<p>Alternatively, if better readability is desired, OP could create their own definition, e.g.:</p>
<pre><code>#define MY_XCB_EVENT_RESPONSE_TYPE_MASK  0x7f  // or (~0x80)
</code></pre>
